{
  "esm": "1.2.0",
  "metadata": {
    "name": "expand-counties-large-runspec",
    "description": "PLAN.md §7.5 step 7d, the GEOGRAPHY dimension at seven: the `expand-counties-large` run computed from the DEFAULT DATABASE through `moves-onroad.esm`. Los Angeles, Miami-Dade, Cook, Washtenaw, New York, Harris and King -- seven counties in seven states, where `runspecs/expand-counties.esm` has three.\n\nWHAT IT DISCRIMINATES, which §23 asks for before a dimension is called covered (PLAN.md §7.3.43).\n\n* SEVEN OF EVERYTHING THE COUNTY KEYS. Seven zones and so seven `zoneMonthHour` temperature and humidity profiles, seven links, seven `zoneRoadType` SHO allocation factors and seven fuel regions -- `regionCounty` puts every county in a region of its own (100000000 for Miami-Dade, 270000000 for Washtenaw, 500000000 for King, 1170011000, 1370011000, 1470011000 and 1570011000 for New York, Harris, Cook and Los Angeles), so the county-keyed `fuelUsageFraction` and the region-keyed fuel supply are read at seven different values. `expand-counties` put three counties in three regions; a join that matched a county to the right region only when the regions were few, or that took a region's FIRST county, would be caught here and not there. I/M does not discriminate: Total Energy Consumption has no I/M adjustment and the snapshot's `IMCoverage` is empty.\n\n* THE COUNTY AXIS WITHOUT A MODEL CHANGE. 875 = 1 x 7 x 1 x 1 x 125 and every cell reproduced on the first run, worst 1.140e-05; the worst cell in each county lies between 6.6e-06 and 1.14e-05, so no county is the outlier. The 270 county-shaped variables `expand-counties` gave the axis are sized by `n_runspeccounty`, discovered from the extent below, and nothing in the model names three.\n\nAgainst `runspecs/expand-counties.esm` this document differs in its fourteen `url_template`s and its notes -- no binding, no key.",
    "authors": [
      "EarthSciML Authors and Contributors"
    ],
    "tags": [
      "runspec",
      "onroad",
      "default-database",
      "expand-counties-large"
    ]
  },
